// The RESULT of `.pop()` at a NUMERIC element rep.
//
//   emitProgram: narrowed union atom has no value box
//
// silent-class inventory D1160. `vl check` returns 0, so this is a clause-2 violation by
// construction — and `print(a.pop())` over an `i32[]` is about as plain as a VL program gets.
//
// THIS LITERAL IS CLAUDE.md's OWN EXAMPLE. D964 cites `narrowed union atom has no value box`
// as the refusal that "reads like an internal invariant, fires on a `vl check`-clean program,
// and is a clause-2 violation the count never saw". It is still live, and this is its witness.
//
// THE STATEMENT FORM RUNS AT EVERY REP — `a.pop()` discarding the result is fine everywhere.
// It is CONSUMING the result that fails, and only at i32 / i64 / f32 / f64; `boolean[]`,
// `u8[]`, `string[]` and a struct list all run. Binding it, printing it, and narrowing it with
// `!= null` fail identically, so the `print` is not the ingredient either.
//
// Controls that already run and must keep running: `a[a.length - 1]` and `a.pop() ?? 0`.
//
// Should print 2 then 1 then 2.5.
const a: i32[] = [1, 2]
print(a.pop())

const b: i64[] = [1]
print(b.pop())

const c: f64[] = [2.5]
print(c.pop())
